SEC. 71101. MORATORIUM ON IMPLEMENTATION OF RULE RELATING TO ELIGIBILITY AND ENROLLMENT IN MEDICARE SAVINGS PROGRAMS.

This section prohibits the Secretary of Health and Human Services from implementing, administering, or enforcing a 2023 final rule that streamlines eligibility and enrollment for Medicare Savings Programs. The moratorium is in effect from the date of enactment until September 30, 2034, and applies to specific sections of the Code of Federal Regulations. It also appropriates $1,000,000 for fiscal year 2026 to the Centers for Medicare & Medicaid Services (CMS) to carry out this section and section 71102.

SEC. 71102. MORATORIUM ON IMPLEMENTATION OF RULE RELATING TO ELIGIBILITY AND ENROLLMENT FOR MEDICAID, CHIP, AND THE BASIC HEALTH PROGRAM.

This section imposes a similar moratorium on a 2024 final rule that streamlines application, eligibility, enrollment, and renewal processes for Medicaid, the Children's Health Insurance Program (CHIP), and the Basic Health Program. The moratorium is in effect from the date of enactment until September 30, 2034, and applies to specific sections of the Code of Federal Regulations. Funding for this section is provided through the appropriation in section 71101.

SEC. 71103. REDUCING DUPLICATE ENROLLMENT UNDER THE MEDICAID AND CHIP PROGRAMS.

This section requires states to establish processes to prevent individuals from being simultaneously enrolled in Medicaid or CHIP in multiple states. By January 1, 2027, one state must have a process to regularly obtain address information for enrollees, and by October 1, 2029, all states must submit monthly data to a new federal system to prevent duplicate enrollment. The Secretary of Health and Human Services is required to establish this system by October 1, 2029, with $10,000,000 appropriated for fiscal year 2026 for its establishment and $20,000,000 for fiscal year 2029 for its maintenance. It also requires managed care entities to transmit address information to states beginning January 1, 2027.

SEC. 71104. ENSURING DECEASED INDIVIDUALS DO NOT REMAIN ENROLLED.

This section requires states, beginning January 1, 2027, to review the Death Master File at least quarterly to identify deceased Medicaid enrollees. States must disenroll deceased individuals and discontinue payments on their behalf, but must reinstate coverage if an individual was erroneously identified as deceased. States may use other electronic data sources to identify deceased beneficiaries as long as they comply with these requirements.

SEC. 71105. ENSURING DECEASED PROVIDERS DO NOT REMAIN ENROLLED.

This section requires states, beginning January 1, 2028, to check the Death Master File when enrolling, reenrolling, or revalidating a provider, and at least quarterly thereafter, to determine if the provider is deceased.

SEC. 71106. PAYMENT REDUCTION RELATED TO CERTAIN ERRONEOUS EXCESS PAYMENTS UNDER MEDICAID.

This section modifies the rules for reducing federal Medicaid payments to states with erroneous excess payments. It clarifies that the Secretary can waive payment reductions for states, but the total amount waived for a fiscal year cannot exceed the amount of erroneous excess payments that exceed the allowable error rate of 0.03. It also expands the definition of erroneous excess payments to include payments for ineligible individuals and payments where eligibility cannot be confirmed. These changes apply beginning with fiscal year 2030.

SEC. 71107. ELIGIBILITY REDETERMINATIONS.

This section requires states to conduct eligibility redeterminations for certain Medicaid expansion individuals (those enrolled under section 1902(a)(10)(A)(i)(VIII)) once every six months, starting after December 31, 2026. It exempts individuals who are medically frail or have special medical needs. The Secretary of Health and Human Services must issue guidance within 180 days, and $75,000,000 is appropriated for fiscal year 2026 for implementation.

SEC. 71108. REVISING HOME EQUITY LIMIT FOR DETERMINING ELIGIBILITY FOR LONG-TERM CARE SERVICES UNDER THE MEDICAID PROGRAM.

This section revises the home equity limit for Medicaid eligibility for long-term care services. States may now apply a higher home equity limit (up to $1,000,000) for homes not zoned for agricultural use, without needing a waiver for statewideness or comparability. For homes on agricultural-zoned lots, the limit is the amount specified in the law. These changes apply beginning January 1, 2028.

SEC. 71109. ALIEN MEDICAID ELIGIBILITY.

This section restricts Medicaid and CHIP payments for medical assistance furnished to aliens, beginning October 1, 2026. Payments will only be made for individuals who are residents of a state or territory and are either citizens, lawful permanent residents, Cuban/Haitian entrants, or individuals residing in the U.S. under a Compact of Free Association. It also appropriates $15,000,000 for fiscal year 2026 for implementation.

SEC. 71110. EXPANSION FMAP FOR EMERGENCY MEDICAID.

This section limits the federal matching rate (FMAP) for emergency Medicaid services furnished to certain aliens, beginning October 1, 2026. The FMAP for these services cannot exceed the state's regular FMAP. It also appropriates $1,000,000 for fiscal year 2026 for implementation.

SEC. 71111. MORATORIUM ON IMPLEMENTATION OF RULE RELATING TO STAFFING STANDARDS FOR LONG-TERM CARE FACILITIES UNDER THE MEDICARE AND MEDICAID PROGRAMS.

This section prohibits the Secretary of Health and Human Services from implementing, administering, or enforcing a 2024 final rule that establishes minimum staffing standards for long-term care facilities. The moratorium is in effect from the date of enactment until September 30, 2034, and applies to specific sections of the Code of Federal Regulations.

SEC. 71112. REDUCING STATE MEDICAID COSTS.

This section reduces the retroactive eligibility period for Medicaid. For expansion individuals, coverage can begin in the month before application, and for all others, it can begin in the second month before application. This applies to applications made on or after the first day of the first quarter after December 31, 2026. It also makes conforming changes to CHIP and appropriates $10,000,000 for fiscal year 2026 for implementation.

SEC. 71113. FEDERAL PAYMENTS TO PROHIBITED ENTITIES.

This section prohibits federal Medicaid funds from being used to make payments to "prohibited entities" for one year after enactment. A prohibited entity is defined as a 501(c)(3) organization that is an essential community provider primarily engaged in family planning, provides abortions (except in cases of rape, incest, or life-endangering conditions), and received over $800,000 in Medicaid payments in fiscal year 2023. It also appropriates $1,000,000 for fiscal year 2026 for implementation.

SEC. 71114. SUNSETTING INCREASED FMAP INCENTIVE.

This section sunsets the increased federal matching rate (FMAP) incentive for states that expand Medicaid. The incentive will only apply to states that began expending amounts for all expansion individuals prior to January 1, 2026.

SEC. 71115. PROVIDER TAXES.

This section changes the threshold for the "hold harmless" provision for broad-based health care related taxes. For fiscal years beginning on or after October 1, 2026, the applicable percent for expansion states will be reduced from 6% to 5.5% in 2028, 5% in 2029, 4.5% in 2030, 4% in 2031, and 3.5% in 2032 and thereafter. Non-expansion states can keep their current tax rates if they were within the hold harmless threshold as of the date of enactment. It also appropriates $20,000,000 for fiscal year 2026 for implementation.

SEC. 71116. STATE DIRECTED PAYMENTS.

This section limits state-directed payments under Medicaid managed care. For expansion states, the total payment rate for a service cannot exceed 100% of the Medicare payment rate, and for non-expansion states, it cannot exceed 110%. Payments with prior approval before May 1, 2025, or for rural hospitals, will be grandfathered but reduced by 10 percentage points each year starting in 2028 until they meet the new limits. It also appropriates $7,000,000 for each of fiscal years 2026 through 2033 for implementation.

SEC. 71117. REQUIREMENTS REGARDING WAIVER OF UNIFORM TAX REQUIREMENT FOR MEDICAID PROVIDER TAX.

This section clarifies the conditions under which a Medicaid provider tax can be considered "generally redistributive" and thus eligible for a waiver of the uniform tax requirement. It defines terms like "Medicaid taxable unit," "non-Medicaid taxable unit," and "tax rate group" and specifies that a tax is not generally redistributive if it imposes lower rates on lower-volume taxpayers or higher rates on Medicaid units. These changes apply only to the 50 states and the District of Columbia.

SEC. 71118. REQUIRING BUDGET NEUTRALITY FOR MEDICAID DEMONSTRATION PROJECTS UNDER SECTION 1115.

This section requires that, beginning January 1, 2027, the Secretary of Health and Human Services cannot approve a Medicaid demonstration project under Section 1115 unless the Chief Actuary of CMS certifies that the project will not increase federal expenditures. It also specifies how savings from a project should be treated in subsequent approval periods. It appropriates $5,000,000 for each of fiscal years 2026 and 2027 for implementation.

SEC. 71119. REQUIREMENT FOR STATES TO ESTABLISH MEDICAID COMMUNITY ENGAGEMENT REQUIREMENTS FOR CERTAIN INDIVIDUALS.

This section requires states to establish community engagement requirements (e.g., working 80 hours per month) as a condition of Medicaid eligibility for certain expansion individuals, beginning no later than the first quarter after December 31, 2026. It includes exceptions for individuals who are medically frail, pregnant, under 19, or over 65, among others. States must provide notice and a 30-day period to comply before disenrolling noncompliant individuals. It also appropriates $200,000,000 for fiscal year 2026 for implementation and $200,000,000 for grants to states to establish necessary systems.

SEC. 71120. MODIFYING COST SHARING REQUIREMENTS FOR CERTAIN EXPANSION INDIVIDUALS UNDER THE MEDICAID PROGRAM.

This section prohibits states from imposing premiums on "specified individuals" (expansion individuals with income above the poverty line) and requires states to impose cost-sharing (e.g., copayments) on them, beginning October 1, 2028. Cost-sharing cannot exceed $35 per item or service, and total family cost-sharing cannot exceed 5% of family income. It also appropriates $15,000,000 for fiscal year 2026 for implementation.

SEC. 71121. MAKING CERTAIN ADJUSTMENTS TO COVERAGE OF HOME OR COMMUNITY-BASED SERVICES UNDER MEDICAID.

This section allows the Secretary of Health and Human Services to approve standalone waivers for home or community-based services (HCBS) for individuals who do not require an institutional level of care, beginning July 1, 2028. States must meet needs-based criteria and demonstrate that the waiver will not increase wait times for other HCBS recipients. It also appropriates $50,000,000 for fiscal year 2026 and $100,000,000 for fiscal year 2027 for implementation and state system support.

SEC. 71201. LIMITING MEDICARE COVERAGE OF CERTAIN INDIVIDUALS.

This section limits Medicare eligibility to U.S. citizens, lawful permanent residents, Cuban/Haitian entrants, and individuals residing in the U.S. under a Compact of Free Association. For individuals already enrolled as of the date of enactment, this restriction applies 18 months later. The Commissioner of Social Security must review current enrollees and notify those who do not meet the new criteria.

SEC. 71202. TEMPORARY PAYMENT INCREASE UNDER THE MEDICARE PHYSICIAN FEE SCHEDULE TO ACCOUNT FOR EXCEPTIONAL CIRCUMSTANCES.

This section provides a temporary 2.5% payment increase under the Medicare Physician Fee Schedule for services furnished in 2026. This is an extension of similar temporary increases provided for 2021 through 2024.

SEC. 71203. EXPANDING AND CLARIFYING THE EXCLUSION FOR ORPHAN DRUGS UNDER THE DRUG PRICE NEGOTIATION PROGRAM.

This section expands the exclusion for orphan drugs under the Medicare Drug Price Negotiation Program. It clarifies that a drug can be excluded if it is designated for one or more rare diseases or conditions, and it extends the exclusion for drugs that were previously designated as orphan drugs but no longer are. These changes apply to initial price applicability years beginning on or after January 1, 2028.

SEC. 71301. PERMITTING PREMIUM TAX CREDIT ONLY FOR CERTAIN INDIVIDUALS.

This section restricts the premium tax credit (PTC) to "eligible aliens," which includes lawful permanent residents, Cuban/Haitian entrants, and individuals residing in the U.S. under a Compact of Free Association. It also requires verification of immigration status for PTC eligibility and applies to taxable years beginning after December 31, 2026.

SEC. 71302. DISALLOWING PREMIUM TAX CREDIT DURING PERIODS OF MEDICAID INELIGIBILITY DUE TO ALIEN STATUS.

This section eliminates the exception that allowed individuals who are ineligible for Medicaid due to their alien status to claim the premium tax credit. This applies to taxable years beginning after December 31, 2025.

SEC. 71303. REQUIRING VERIFICATION OF ELIGIBILITY FOR PREMIUM TAX CREDIT.

This section requires Exchanges to verify an individual's eligibility for enrollment and advance payments of the premium tax credit before a month can be considered a "coverage month." It also requires Exchanges to provide a pre-enrollment verification process for household income and eligibility. These changes apply to taxable years beginning after December 31, 2027.

SEC. 71304. DISALLOWING PREMIUM TAX CREDIT IN CASE OF CERTAIN COVERAGE ENROLLED IN DURING SPECIAL ENROLLMENT PERIOD.

This section disallows the premium tax credit for coverage enrolled in during a special enrollment period that is based on income and not connected to a specific event or change in circumstances. This applies to plan years beginning after December 31, 2025.

SEC. 71305. ELIMINATING LIMITATION ON RECAPTURE OF ADVANCE PAYMENT OF PREMIUM TAX CREDIT.

This section eliminates the limitation on the recapture of excess advance payments of the premium tax credit. This means individuals may have to repay the full amount of any excess advance payments, rather than a capped amount. This applies to taxable years beginning after December 31, 2025.

SEC. 71306. PERMANENT EXTENSION OF SAFE HARBOR FOR ABSENCE OF DEDUCTIBLE FOR TELEHEALTH SERVICES.

This section makes permanent the safe harbor that allows high deductible health plans (HDHPs) to cover telehealth services before the deductible is met. This applies to plan years beginning after December 31, 2024.

SEC. 71307. ALLOWANCE OF BRONZE AND CATASTROPHIC PLANS IN CONNECTION WITH HEALTH SAVINGS ACCOUNTS.

This section allows bronze and catastrophic health plans offered through an Exchange to be treated as high deductible health plans (HDHPs) for purposes of health savings accounts (HSAs). This applies to months beginning after December 31, 2025.

SEC. 71308. TREATMENT OF DIRECT PRIMARY CARE SERVICE ARRANGEMENTS.

This section clarifies that direct primary care service arrangements are not treated as health plans for HSA purposes, and the fees for such arrangements are considered medical expenses. It limits the monthly fee for such arrangements to $150 per individual (or $300 for a family) and excludes certain services like procedures requiring general anesthesia and most prescription drugs. This applies to months beginning after December 31, 2025.

SEC. 71401. RURAL HEALTH TRANSFORMATION PROGRAM.

This section establishes a new Rural Health Transformation Program, appropriating $10 billion per year for fiscal years 2026 through 2030 to provide allotments to the 50 states. States must submit an application with a detailed rural health transformation plan by December 31, 2025, and can use funds for activities like improving prevention, supporting technology, recruiting clinicians, and assisting rural communities. It also appropriates $200,000,000 for fiscal year 2025 for implementation.

SEC. 72001. MODIFICATION OF LIMITATION ON THE PUBLIC DEBT.

This section increases the public debt limit by $5,000,000,000,000.

SEC. 73001. ENDING UNEMPLOYMENT PAYMENTS TO JOBLESS MILLIONAIRES.

This section prohibits the use of federal funds to pay unemployment compensation to individuals whose base period wages equal or exceed $1,000,000. It requires applicants to self-certify their income and state agencies to verify wage eligibility and recover any overpayments. This applies to weeks of unemployment beginning on or after the date of enactment. [end of text]


